#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[ABOUT THIS BOOKLET])
#v(0.08in)

Athanasius (c. 296-373) was bishop of Alexandria for forty-five years and
spent nearly twenty of them in exile, five times driven out for defending
the faith of Nicaea, that the Son is truly God. He is remembered for _On the
Incarnation_ and for his _Life of Antony_, which made the desert monks known
to the whole Church.

This letter is gentler. A friend, Marcellinus, has been ill and is reading
the Psalms closely, trying to grasp the meaning of each one. Athanasius
answers by passing on what a wise old man once taught him. The Psalter, the
old man says, holds the whole of Scripture in song; it is a mirror of the
soul; and unlike the rest of Scripture, its words can be spoken as our own.
Then he does something no one had done before: he goes through life's
troubles and joys one by one and names the psalm to pray in each.

Read it slowly, with a Bible open. When a psalm is named, turn to it.

This is the first booklet in the collection _On Prayer_.

#pagebreak()
#align(center, text(size: 7pt, tracking: 0.12em)[ABOUT THIS TRANSLATION])
#v(0.08in)

The English versions in print are still under copyright, so this booklet
has its own, made from the Greek. It aims to be plain and faithful rather
than elegant.

*Psalm numbers.* Athanasius used the Greek Bible, whose numbering of the
Psalms runs one behind ours for most of the book. The numbers here have been
changed to match English Bibles, so you can look them up directly.

*Psalm wording.* His quotations follow the Greek, so they will sometimes
read differently from your Bible. Psalm 151, which he mentions, is found
only in the Greek Bible.

*The old man.* Most of the letter is his teaching, reported by Athanasius;
the voice changes only at the beginning and, briefly, near the end.

*The headings* in italics are the editor's, added to help you find your
way. The letter itself runs on without them.
